th tommy um  here at mit and uh this game came out  called foldit some of you might remember  uh it came out i think in roughly 2008  from baker lab and it was um really i  think the best and probably still the  best example of what one would call a  citizen science game so what happened  with this game folded is that they uh  the baker lab turned the fold of the  protein folding problem into a puzzle  game  pretty pretty esoteric but quite fun  puzzle game for those of that persuasion  and um they actually you know got lots  and lots of gamers to play this game and  actually choose to make bends in the  backbone of the um  of the uh of the protein and you get an  estimate of the uh energy score right  basically and you're trying to minimize  this free energy um  what was really cool about that is that  uh well firstly i was fascinated by the  idea of you know the general public  having fun with the game but really they  were contributing to science which um  seems like you know if we could actually  tap that that would be pretty cool um  for gamers and for science i think uh  and uh so i was pretty fascinated by  that idea but also i was quite intrigued  that the best players um of uh fold it  game actually found some real structures  and actually some apparently some pretty  important ones that were published in  nature and natural structural molecular  biology in the in the subsequent years  and um  and so i was sort of thinking as i was  watching this you know looking at this  in 2010 roughly that kind of time well  what's what's happened there you know  how is this possible and you know when  you look into the kind of moves that the  players made they were making you know  local moves that were actually  increasing the energy you know the free  energy for the moment because overall  they realized later on they might be  able to  solve that you know actually globally  they like that would be lower energy and  this is the reason why greedy uh greedy  energy minimization uh uh  methods won't don't work because they  just get stuck into you know local  maxima  and so  so this uh so they're obviously able to  somehow sort of use their intuition i  mean i guess the pattern matching of  these gamers they were they weren't  biologists they were just gamers but  some of them are really good at pattern  matching and they presumably were using  that uh to sort of find their way their  intuition if you like uh to find their  way to a good fold  so then later on so that was sort of  filed away too and then later on when we  when we beat lisa dole in the soul match  um and i sort of thought back to and in  the process of that what what what were  we doing really with alphago is we  mimicked um the intuition of these go  masters right and and believe me if you  talk to these go masters you know that's  all they've ever done in their lives  like play go from the age of you know  two or three years old go to go school  then go to normal school that's you  cannot get a more highly trained mind  for a particular task than a go master  right literally i think it's kind of  impossible so if we were able to mimic  their intuition in go then why would it  not be possible to sort of do something  analogous in this protein space so that  gave me the feeling that this was um  definitely a crackable problem uh with  our techniques  so we actually started the alpha fall  project  almost the day after we got back off the  plane from seoul  with alphago and uh we actually found a  bit of recording somewhere where i was  talking to dave silva who is the project  leader now for go out in seoul and i  think we must have just won the third  match or something and we were literally  discussing it as like i think now is the  time to  push forward on on the protein folding  project  so that's what we did  another really uh uh important part of  the story was that the existence of this  competition called casp critical  assessment of protein structure  prediction and it's an amazing  competition it's sort of it's considered  to be like the olympics for protein  folding and it runs every two years uh  and it's run by this amazing group of  organizers who've been running it since  1994 so they've diligently kept this  going for close to 30 years now and what  they do is um is that as you know every  throughout a year uh experimental  departments produce new crystals and an  experimental uh determined structures of  proteins right  but over a kind of three month period  what they do is they they hold back  publishing those results  and they instead submit their newly  freshly found structures into this  competition  but it's a blind assessment so the  people the the the computational teams  that that compete in this uh uh  competition don't um know the structure  nobody in fact knows the structure other  than the group the experimental group uh  that had just found it um and then you  get you know it's quite exciting you  sort of get this uh a sequence emailed  to you and you have like one week to get  back with your best guess of the  structure uh and it's pretty fun and  it's all you do over the summer  and then at the end of all that the  organizers you know they release the  real structures and they measure how  close uh were your predictor structures  uh of all the different teams to the to  the uh to the experimentally determined  ground truth  and so you know i wanted to give a big  shout out to john malt who is the  founder of this and and the other cat  organizers who worked so hard over many  many years to um to maintain this  competition i actually think it's pretty  good model for  helping science to progress and maybe  should be thought about in other domains  too  so historically then what have been  happening at casp well it turned out  when you look back on it that  there'd been sort of very little  progress at cast for over a decade  so here on on the on on this graph uh  i'm showing you the the the winning  the the median accuracy for the winning  team at each subsequent casper  competition starting in 2006 with cash 7  and going to 2016 with casp12  and on the y-axis you can see it's a  measure of the accuracy um that the  winning team got and it's um it's called  gdt i won't go into details of how it's  calculated but you can sort of think  about you can roughly speaking it's the  percentage of residues that you have  accurately um put in the right position  within a certain tolerance certain  distance tolerance right  so obviously you're trying to get close  to 100 if you can  um  and you can see here that basically  they've been almost no progress and they  were stuck around this 40 level that's  basically a kind of useless structure so  um so this was sort of stagnated and  then we came along in uh 2018 which is  the first one we entered after working  on alpha fall for a couple of years with  alpha fold one and we made this huge  leap um and i think it kind of  revolutionized the field so we were  first it was the first time really that  um cutting-edge machine learning had  been applied at scale as the core  component of a solution um uh to the  protein folding problem and uh and alpha  for one won the competition but not only  did it win it won by almost 50  above the next best team so um and and  and you can see so that would have meant  that the teams were still tracking along  the gray bars um if it wasn't for this  new uh advance  and then of course that's not where we  wanted to stop we wanted to actually  solve the problem  and um and we had in in 2020 we actually  re-architected the whole system for the  2020 competition to reach atomic  accuracy uh and that was alpha fall two  so that was the and again another even  bigger leap um  above uh alpha fold one  so these were the results in terms of  accuracy so after fall to sort of  achieve this atomic accuracy uh at cast  14  and we were told by the organizers um  back in 2016-17 that the magic threshold  that they considered  to be the sort of threshold that needed  to be crossed was less than one angstrom  error  um that was the kind of critical  threshold for it to be competitive with  experimental methods and therefore  useful in practice so that's what we're  always aiming for so less than one  action so atomic accuracy basically the  width of a carbon atom  and alpha volt2 crossed that line and  actually achieved a 0.96 angstrom error  in the cast 14 competition for the first  time ever and you can see this was still  actually three times more accurate  than the next best systems from um some  commercial ones like from tencent but  also the best labs that work in this  area by collab zang lab and so on which  were still around the three angstrom era  in cast 14 even though they'd already  incorporated the alpha fold one advances  that obviously we'd um published um  after cast 13.  so this led to the organizers and john  malt and the organizers declaring the  structured prediction problem uh  protein folding essentially sort of  solved  and here's some examples of the outputs  of alpha fold many of you have seen this  by now but this was actually um  incredibly sort of wonderful for us to  see this uh when we got given back the  results from cast 14 and you can see how  good our predictions are the ones in  blue and the ground truth is in green  and they're overlaid over each other and  you can see that they're highly um  overlapping um including on the left  hand side this is a protein from sars uh  that even the side chains so these  little thin stalks  which are the side chains not on the  backbone of the protein  are accurately predicted too  oh sorry and um  this is the architecture for alpha fold  too um so there's there's a whole bunch  of innovations that were required here i  haven't got time to go all into all of  them but i'll give you the kind of key  takeaways if you're interested in the um  in the details there you can check out  our nature paper that has about 60 pages  of supplemental information so it's um  so maybe just a measure of how how  complex this system was  and it was by far the most complicated  system that we have ever built at  deepmind and we built some pretty  complicated systems but this is i would  say the most complex one ever  there were actually 32 component  algorithms that went into alpha fold  as i mentioned the 60 pages of si and if  there was no real silver bullet in the  end that solved protein folding actually  needed a whole host of insights and  innovations and then those innovations  had to be carefully integrated together  but the key takeaways were and the key  advances of alpha fold 2 compared to  alpha fold 1 was that firstly it was an  end-to-end system with a kind of  iterative recycling stage that actually  builds the structure piece by piece um  and builds that out and that allowed us  to go directly from um  the amino acid sequence directly to the  3d structure with alpha fold one there  was an uh intermediate step which is  that we went from the amino acid  sequence to what's called a distrogram  so a pairwise distrogram of all the  residues to all the residues and then  there was a kind of newton uh newton  method to optimize that numerical method  um that actually took the longest part  of it it took like one week per per  protein um  and both those two uh  systems had to be optimized separately  and those of you work at machine  learning will know uh be familiar with  the idea that the more end to end you  can make things generally the better it  is the kind of optimization that you do  seems to flow better and that was  definitely the case here when we were  able to optimize directly for the 3d  structure  we also used an attention-based neural  network rather than convolutional neural  network and that was key because we were  using a convolutional neural network in  in for alpha 4.1 and of course if you  think about it that's the wrong type of  bias right like obviously pixels uh on  an image if they're nearby they're  pretty correlated in their properties  but actually in fact you know that's not  true at all necessarily of of amino  acids uh that are near each other in the  sequence right obviously it completely  depends on how um the backbone uh is  going to bend round  so um so that this this attention-based  neural network would sort of infer the  implicit graph structure and then  finally we were able to build in and  this was critical evolutionary and  physics physical constraints into the  model architecture  without impacting the training and the  learning right so um uh that was you  know pretty difficult to sort of  building these constraints without  hurting the learning um but in the end  that turned out to be totally essential  so it's a kind of huge research effort  um took over five years at its peak  about 20 people highly  multi-disciplinary team so we have lots  of biologists and chemists and  physicists on the team as well as  obviously machine learning and  engineering  and the other thing to note is if the  output of a particular applied  system is in itself really valuable like  we thought this would be for biologists  then one maybe you should relax the  constraint of being general so normally  with all our machine learning research  we try and be as general as possible but  here we wanted to just solve the problem  by any means so we used all of our  general  purpose knowledge and general purpose  algorithms but then we actually built in  quite a lot of domain knowledge in this  case like the evolutionary physical  constraints  because we just wanted to solve  the problem here  in terms of speed and scale  actually this system turns out to be  incredibly fast  especially inference  so it takes about two weeks on eight  tpus roughly 150 gpus to  train  so actually fairly modest amount of  compute by today's standards uh and um  the inference is super fast so order of  minutes for an average pro